"Stand in the sunshine at noon on a summer day, and you can feel the heat on your skin. Stand in the same sunshine late in the afternoon, and it feels much gentler, even though it is the same Sun. What changed is the angle of the light. The same idea explains why the lands near the equator are hot and the lands near the poles are cold.": "806d57d51af6",
"Stand in the sunshine at noon on a summer day, and you can feel the heat on your skin. Stand in the same sunshine late in the afternoon, and it feels much gentler, even though it is the same Sun. What changed is the angle of the light. The same idea explains why the lands near the equator are hot and the lands near the poles are cold. Try it with a flashlight. Shine it straight down at a table, and you get a small, bright spot. Tilt it, and the light stretches into a longer, dimmer patch. The flashlight gives out the same amount of light both times. When it is tilted, that light is spread over more of the table, so each part of the table gets less. Sunlight reaches the round Earth in the same way. Near the equator, the noon Sun is high and its rays come down almost straight. Toward the poles, the Earth curves away, and the same sunlight arrives at a slant. NASA explains that from the equator to the poles, sunlight meets the Earth at smaller and smaller angles, and “the light gets spread over larger and larger surface areas.” Slanted sunlight also travels a longer path through the air before it reaches the ground. The Earth's axis is tilted, so the place where the noon Sun is straight overhead moves during the year. In June it is over the Tropic of Cancer, about 23.4 degrees north. In December it is over the Tropic of Capricorn, about 23.4 degrees south. Outside the tropics, the noon Sun is never straight overhead. Beyond the Arctic and Antarctic Circles, there are days in winter when the Sun does not rise at all. These four lines divide the Earth into five zones: the tropical zone between the two tropics, two temperate zones, and two polar zones beyond the polar circles. Real climates are more complicated, because oceans, winds, mountains, and height above sea level all make a difference. But the angle of sunlight is the starting point. The Tropic of Cancer crosses Taiwan, passing through Chiayi, Hualien, and Penghu. Taiwan's Central Weather Administration says this is why southern Taiwan is tropical and northern Taiwan is subtropical. In June, the noon Sun stands almost straight above the Tropic of Cancer markers. When you feel the summer sun in Taiwan, you are feeling sunlight that is nearly as direct as sunlight can be.": "7e15c1ebf4fd",
"Sunlight reaches the round Earth in the same way. Near the equator, the noon Sun is high and its rays come down almost straight. Toward the poles, the Earth curves away, and the same sunlight arrives at a slant. NASA explains that from the equator to the poles, sunlight meets the Earth at smaller and smaller angles, and “the light gets spread over larger and larger surface areas.” Slanted sunlight also travels a longer path through the air before it reaches the ground.": "60a53381ac1e",
"The Earth's axis is tilted, so the place where the noon Sun is straight overhead moves during the year. In June it is over the Tropic of Cancer, about 23.4 degrees north. In December it is over the Tropic of Capricorn, about 23.4 degrees south. Outside the tropics, the noon Sun is never straight overhead. Beyond the Arctic and Antarctic Circles, there are days in winter when the Sun does not rise at all.": "6aa1269358b5",
"The Sun is high in the sky near the equator.": "57e8ca358ec9",
